<title>GERMANY: German DTB readies switch to euro if Germany joins.</title>
<headline>German DTB readies switch to euro if Germany joins.</headline>
<dateline>FRANKFURT 1997-03-14</dateline>
<text>
<p>Germany's DTB electronic financial futures exchange said on Friday that it would switch its contracts to euros if Germany joined Europe's currency union in 1999.</p>
<p>A DTB spokesman said the exchange planned to make the move only if Germany actually joined currency union in January 1999 but intended to inform investors that it was prepared to switch contract specifications.</p>
<p>If Germany joins the curreny union, all contracts that expire after 1999 would be based on the three-month Euro-LIBOR rather than three-month DM-LIBOR, the exchange said.</p>
<p>The three-month-Euro-LIBOR, like the three-month DM-LIBOR contract, will be calculated by the British Bankers' Association, the DTB said.</p>
<p>--Frankfurt Newsroom, +49 69 756525</p>
</text>
